ek","dish/makanek"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Cocido","dish/cocido"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Cocido","dish/cocido"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Rakott krumpli","dish/rakott-krumpli"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Lunch meat","ingredient/lunch-meat"],["Phaseolus vulgaris","ingredient/phaseolus-vulgaris"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Hungarian sausages","ingredient/hungarian-sausages"],["Sour cream","ingredient/sour-cream"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/f3/Sa%C5%82o.JPG/500px-Sa%C5%82o.JPG","w":500,"h":427,"by":"Beiot","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sa%C5%82o.JPG"}},"dish/digestive-biscuit":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/digestive-biscuit","n":"Digestive biscuit","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from British cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A digestive biscuit, sometimes described as a sweet-meal biscuit, is a semi-sweet biscuit that originated in Scotland. The digestive was first developed in 1839 by two doctors to aid digestion. The term digestive is derived from the belief, around the time the biscuit was first introduced, that they had antacid properties due to the use of sodium bicarbonate as an ingredient. Historically, some producers used diastatic malt extract to \"digest\" some of the starch that existed in flour prior to baking."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-digestive-biscuit"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digestive_biscuit"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":9,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Leavening agent","ingredient/leavening-agent"],["Malic acid","ingredient/malic-acid"],["Malt extract","ingredient/malt-extract"],["Sodium bicarbonate","ingredient/sodium-bicarbonate"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tartaric acid","ingredient/tartaric-acid"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Wholemeal","ingredient/wholemeal"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Great British Chefs: traditional British recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatbritishchefs.com","note":"UK chef-driven recipe site with a large section of classic British dishes.","url":"https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/features/our-most-popular-traditional-british-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from British cuisine","links":[["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Battenberg cake","dish/battenberg-cake"],["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"],["Bread sauce","dish/bread-sauce"],["Carpetbag steak","dish/carpetbag-steak"],["Chelsea bun","dish/chelsea-bun"],["Christmas pudding","dish/christmas-pudding"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Crumble","dish/crumble"],["Crumpet","dish/crumpet"],["Curry in the United Kingdom","dish/curry-in-the-united-kingdom"],["Dundee cake","dish/dundee-cake"],["English muffin","dish/english-muffin"],["Flies' graveyard","dish/flies-graveyard"],["Flummery","dish/flummery"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"],["Garibaldi biscuit","dish/garibaldi-biscuit"],["Hot cross bun","dish/hot-cross-bun"],["Hovis biscuit","dish/hovis-biscuit"],["Jammie Dodgers","dish/jammie-dodgers"],["Melba toast","dish/melba-toast"],["Mince pie","dish/mince-pie"],["Phall","dish/phall"],["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Ploughman's lunch","dish/ploughman-s-lunch"],["Potted shrimps","dish/potted-shrimps"],["Prawn cocktail, steak and Black Forest gateau","dish/prawn-cocktail-steak-and-black-forest-gateau"],["Rich tea","dish/rich-tea"],["Sausage roll","dish/sausage-roll"],["Scotch egg","dish/scotch-egg"],["Shepherd's pie","dish/shepherd-s-pie"],["Soldiers (food)","dish/soldiers-food"],["Spotted dick","dish/spotted-dick"],["Tea sandwich","dish/tea-sandwich"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/47/Digestive_biscuits.jpg/500px-Digestive_biscuits.jpg","w":500,"h":329,"by":"User:Caesura","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Digestive_biscuits.jpg"}},"ingredient/seville-orange":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/seville-orange","n":"Seville orange","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["The bitter orange, sour orange, Seville orange, bigarade orange, or marmalade orange is the hybrid citrus tree species Citrus × aurantium, and its fruit. It is native to Southeast Asia and has been spread by humans to many parts of the world. It is a cross between the pomelo, Citrus maxima, and the wild type mandarin orange, Citrus reticulata. The bitter orange is used to make essential oil, used in foods, drinks, and pharmaceuticals. The Seville orange is prized for making British orange marmalade."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-seville-orange"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seville_orange"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Dundee cake","dish/dundee-cake"]]],["Maya cuisine","cuisine/maya-cuisine",[["Cochinita pibil","dish/cochinita-pibil"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Currant","ingredient/currant"],["Sultana (grape)","ingredient/sultana-grape"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Achiote","ingredient/annatto"],["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/2/2e/Citrus_aurantium.jpg/500px-Citrus_aurantium.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"A. Barra","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citrus_aurantium.jpg"}},"dish/ghonghi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ghonghi","n":"Ghonghi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Nepalese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Ghonghi is a Nepalese freshwater snail dish prepared by the Tharu and Madheshi people of southern Nepal. It is eaten by sucking the snail from its shell and is found throughout the Madhesh Province and Terai districts of other states. It is also popular among Rajbanshi, Dhimals, Santhal and Danuwar people of Terai."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ghonghi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghonghi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Nepalese cuisine","cuisine/nepalese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Flaxseed","ingredient/flaxseed"],["Freshwater snail","ingredient/freshwater-snail"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Gundruk","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thegundruk.com","note":"Blog dedicated to Nepali recipes and food culture.","url":"https://thegundruk.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Nepalese cuisine","links":[["Dhikri","dish/dhikri"],["Mokthuk","dish/mokthuk"],["Momo (food)","dish/momo-food"],["Sel roti","dish/sel-roti"],["Sohari","dish/sohari"],["Thali","dish/thali"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/bd/Ghogi.jpg/500px-Ghogi.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Janak Bhatta","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghogi.jpg"}},"ingredient/cornstarch":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cornstarch","n":"Cornstarch","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Corn starch, or cornflour (British English) is the starch powder derived from corn grain. The starch is obtained from the endosperm of the kernel."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cornstarch"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornstarch"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantones